\section{Why $\Omega$ Cannot Be a Diagonal Matrix}
% =============================================================================

A natural simplification would be to set $\Omega = \mathrm{diag}(\omega_1,\ldots,\omega_5)$,
so that each aggregate component is merely a rescaled version of exactly one structural
shock.  We argue---on identification, economic, and statistical grounds---that this
restriction is \textbf{invalid}.

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Argument 1: Observational Non-Identification}
% ─────────────────────────────────────────────

\begin{proposition}[Non-Identification under Diagonal $\Omega$]
If $\Omega$ is restricted to be diagonal, then $A_t$ and $\Omega$ cannot be
separately identified from the reduced-form response matrix
$B_t = A_t\Omega$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $\Omega = D \equiv \mathrm{diag}(\omega_1,\ldots,\omega_5)$.  Then
\[
  B_t = A_t D.
\]
For any scalar $c_k \neq 0$, replace $\omega_k \to c_k\omega_k$ and
$a_{\cdot k} \to a_{\cdot k}/c_k$ (rescale the $k$-th column of $A_t$
by $1/c_k$).  The product $B_t$ is unchanged:
\[
  \tilde A_t \tilde D
  = \left(\frac{A_t}{c_k}\right) (c_k D)
  = A_t D = B_t.
\]
Since infinitely many pairs $(\tilde A_t, \tilde D)$ produce the same observable
$B_t$, neither $A_t$ nor $\Omega$ is identified separately.  A normalization
such as $\omega_k = 1$ for all $k$ is required---which trivially collapses
$\Omega$ to the identity and makes the matrix redundant.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The identification problem vanishes when $\Omega$ has \emph{off-diagonal} elements,
because these cross-loadings impose non-trivial cross-equation restrictions that
pin down $\Omega$ separately from $A_t$.
\end{remark}

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Argument 2: Economic Cross-Shock Transmission}
% ─────────────────────────────────────────────

A diagonal $\Omega$ asserts that shock $k$ affects \emph{only} aggregate component
$k$ and has \textbf{zero influence} on all other aggregate channels.  This is
economically implausible for our five shocks:

\bigskip
\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.5}
\begin{tabular}{p{3.2cm} p{9cm}}
\toprule
\textbf{Shock} & \textbf{Cross-channel transmission (off-diagonal $\omega_{ij}\neq 0$)} \\
\midrule
Oil Price ($\varepsilon^{\mathrm{Oil}}$)
  & Raises marginal costs $\Rightarrow$ central bank raises rates
    ($\varepsilon^{\mathrm{MP}}$ channel activated).
    Also reduces TFP in energy-intensive sectors
    ($\varepsilon^{\mathrm{TFP}}$ channel activated). \\
Financial Crisis ($\varepsilon^{\mathrm{Crisis}}$)
  & Triggers emergency fiscal expansions ($\varepsilon^{\mathrm{FP}}$),
    extraordinary monetary easing ($\varepsilon^{\mathrm{MP}}$), and
    supply-side disruptions ($\varepsilon^{\mathrm{TFP}}$)
    \emph{simultaneously}. \\
Monetary Policy ($\varepsilon^{\mathrm{MP}}$)
  & Rate changes alter government borrowing costs, feeding back into
    fiscal dynamics ($\varepsilon^{\mathrm{FP}}$ channel). \\
Fiscal Policy ($\varepsilon^{\mathrm{FP}}$)
  & Government spending shocks affect aggregate demand and signal future
    monetary accommodation ($\varepsilon^{\mathrm{MP}}$ channel). \\
TFP ($\varepsilon^{\mathrm{TFP}}$)
  & Productivity gains alter the natural rate of interest and thus
    optimal monetary policy ($\varepsilon^{\mathrm{MP}}$ channel). \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\bigskip\noindent
These are not exotic second-order effects; they are first-order channels documented
in the VAR and DSGE literatures (Bernanke, Gertler \& Watson 1997; Blanchard \&
Gal\'i 2007; Ramey 2011).  Setting $\omega_{ij}=0$ for $i\neq j$ imposes that all
such channels are absent, a restriction flatly rejected by the data.

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Argument 3: Statistical Rejection via Covariance Structure}
% ─────────────────────────────────────────────

\begin{lemma}[Off-Diagonal Covariance Implies Non-Diagonal $\Omega$]
Suppose the structural shocks are orthonormal, $\Sigma_\varepsilon = I_5$.
If the observed covariance matrix of $\aggr_t$ has any non-zero off-diagonal
elements, then $\Omega$ cannot be diagonal.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
From equation~\eqref{eq:cov_aggr} with $\Sigma_\varepsilon = I_5$:
\[
  \Sigma_{\aggr} = \Omega\Omega^{\top}.
\]
If $\Omega = D$ is diagonal, then $DD^{\top} = \mathrm{diag}(\omega_1^2,\ldots,\omega_5^2)$
is also diagonal---all off-diagonal entries of $\Sigma_{\aggr}$ would be zero.
Hence any empirically observed $[\Sigma_{\aggr}]_{ij}\neq 0$ for $i\neq j$
is a direct contradiction of $\Omega$ being diagonal.
\end{proof}

\noindent In practice, the five macroeconomic shocks co-move (e.g., crises cluster with
monetary easing, oil shocks co-occur with recessions), so the sample covariance of the
estimated shock series will have non-zero off-diagonal terms, directly rejecting
$H_0: \Omega = \mathrm{diag}(\cdot)$.

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Argument 4: PCA Generically Yields a Non-Diagonal $\Omega$}
% ─────────────────────────────────────────────

A natural estimation strategy is to extract $\Omega$ as the matrix of eigenvectors
of the cross-shock covariance matrix (Principal Component Analysis).  Let
$\Sigma_\varepsilon = V\Lambda V^{\top}$ be the eigen-decomposition, where $\Lambda$
is diagonal and $V$ is the orthogonal matrix of eigenvectors.  Setting
$\Omega = V^{\top}$ yields orthonormal aggregate components:

\begin{equation}
  \Sigma_{\aggr}
  = \Omega\Sigma_\varepsilon\Omega^{\top}
  = V^{\top} V \Lambda V^{\top} V
  = \Lambda \quad (\text{diagonal}).
\end{equation}

\noindent However, $V^{\top}$ is \emph{generically non-diagonal}: each principal
component loads on all five shocks unless the shocks happen to arrive already
orthogonal with zero cross-covariance---an assumption that must be imposed, not
discovered.  Thus the PCA estimator of $\Omega$ is non-diagonal unless a very
special and implausible structure holds.

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Summary: Why $\Omega$ Must Be a Full Matrix}
% ─────────────────────────────────────────────

\begin{keybox}[Conclusion: $\Omega$ Is Non-Diagonal]
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Identification}: Diagonal $\Omega$ is not separately identified
        from $A_t$; the model is under-determined.
  \item \textbf{Economics}: Cross-shock transmission---oil $\to$ MP,
        crisis $\to$ FP + MP + TFP, fiscal $\to$ MP---is
        empirically established and inconsistent with $\omega_{ij}=0$.
  \item \textbf{Statistics}: Non-zero off-diagonal entries in the
        covariance of $\aggr_t$ (observable) directly reject the diagonal
        restriction.
  \item \textbf{PCA}: The eigenvector matrix of any non-diagonal
        $\Sigma_\varepsilon$ is generically a full matrix, giving a
        non-diagonal $\Omega$.
\end{enumerate}
Therefore, $\Omega\in\R^{5\times 5}$ must be treated as a \textbf{full,
unrestricted matrix}, and its estimation must exploit the cross-shock
covariance structure.
\end{keybox}

% =============================================================================
\section{Explicit Multiplication: $A_t\,\Omega \to B_t$ and $B_t\,\bm{\varepsilon}_t \to \mathbf{Y}_t$}
% =============================================================================

We now carry out the multiplication step by step.

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Step 1 --- Multiply $A_t\,(2\times5)$ by $\Omega\,(5\times5)$}
% ─────────────────────────────────────────────

\begin{equation}\label{eq:AtOmega}
  A_t\,\Omega
  =
  \begin{pmatrix}
    a_{11} & a_{12} & a_{13} & a_{14} & a_{15}\\[4pt]
    a_{21} & a_{22} & a_{23} & a_{24} & a_{25}
  \end{pmatrix}
  \begin{pmatrix}
    \omega_{11} & \omega_{12} & \omega_{13} & \omega_{14} & \omega_{15}\\[4pt]
    \omega_{21} & \omega_{22} & \omega_{23} & \omega_{24} & \omega_{25}\\[4pt]
    \omega_{31} & \omega_{32} & \omega_{33} & \omega_{34} & \omega_{35}\\[4pt]
    \omega_{41} & \omega_{42} & \omega_{43} & \omega_{44} & \omega_{45}\\[4pt]
    \omega_{51} & \omega_{52} & \omega_{53} & \omega_{54} & \omega_{55}
  \end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}

The general $(i,j)$ entry of the product is:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bij_general}
  [A_t\Omega]_{ij}
  \;=\;
  \sum_{k=1}^{5} a_{ik}\,\omega_{kj},
  \qquad i=1,2;\quad j=1,\ldots,5.
\end{equation}

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Step 2 --- The Resulting $2\times5$ Matrix $B_t$}
% ─────────────────────────────────────────────

Writing out all ten entries explicitly:

\begin{keybox}[Reduced-Form Response Matrix $B_t = A_t\Omega \;\in\;\R^{2\times5}$]
{\small
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Bt_explicit}
  B_t =
  \begin{pmatrix}
    \displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{5}a_{1k}\omega_{k1} &
    \displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{5}a_{1k}\omega_{k2} &
    \displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{5}a_{1k}\omega_{k3} &
    \displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{5}a_{1k}\omega_{k4} &
    \displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{5}a_{1k}\omega_{k5} \\[12pt]
    \displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{5}a_{2k}\omega_{k1} &
    \displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{5}a_{2k}\omega_{k2} &
    \displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{5}a_{2k}\omega_{k3} &
    \displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{5}a_{2k}\omega_{k4} &
    \displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{5}a_{2k}\omega_{k5}
  \end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}
}
\end{keybox}

\noindent Expanding each entry in full (row 1 $=$ log IP; row 2 $=$ unemployment):

\begin{align}
  b_{1j} &= a_{11}\omega_{1j} + a_{12}\omega_{2j} + a_{13}\omega_{3j}
           + a_{14}\omega_{4j} + a_{15}\omega_{5j},
  \quad j=1,\ldots,5, \label{eq:b1j}\\[6pt]
  b_{2j} &= a_{21}\omega_{1j} + a_{22}\omega_{2j} + a_{23}\omega_{3j}
           + a_{24}\omega_{4j} + a_{25}\omega_{5j},
  \quad j=1,\ldots,5. \label{eq:b2j}
\end{align}

Labelling the columns by their corresponding structural shock:

\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.5}
\begin{tabular}{cccccc}
\toprule
 & $j=1$ (FP) & $j=2$ (MP) & $j=3$ (TFP) & $j=4$ (Oil) & $j=5$ (Crisis)\\
\midrule
$b_{1j}$ & total IP response & to FP shock & to TFP shock & to Oil shock & to Crisis\\
$b_{2j}$ & total $u$ response & to FP shock & to TFP shock & to Oil shock & to Crisis\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\bigskip\noindent
\textbf{Key insight:} each $b_{ij}$ is a \emph{weighted sum over all five rows of $\Omega$},
mixing every structural shock channel.  This confirms that $\Omega$ cannot be
diagonal---otherwise $b_{ij} = a_{ij}\omega_{jj}$ and all cross-channel information
is lost.

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Step 3 --- Multiply $B_t\,(2\times5)$ by $\bm{\varepsilon}_t\,(5\times1)$}
% ─────────────────────────────────────────────

\begin{keybox}[Final Output: $\mathbf{Y}_t = B_t\,\bm{\varepsilon}_t \;\in\;\R^{2\times1}$]
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Yt_final}
  \begin{pmatrix} y_{1,t}\\[6pt] y_{2,t} \end{pmatrix}
  =
  \begin{pmatrix}
    b_{11} & b_{12} & b_{13} & b_{14} & b_{15}\\[4pt]
    b_{21} & b_{22} & b_{23} & b_{24} & b_{25}
  \end{pmatrix}
  \begin{pmatrix}
    \varepsilon_{t}^{\mathrm{FP}}\\[4pt]
    \varepsilon_{t}^{\mathrm{MP}}\\[4pt]
    \varepsilon_{t}^{\mathrm{TFP}}\\[4pt]
    \varepsilon_{t}^{\mathrm{Oil}}\\[4pt]
    \varepsilon_{t}^{\mathrm{Crisis}}
  \end{pmatrix}
  +
  \begin{pmatrix}\omega_{1,t}\\[6pt]\omega_{2,t}\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}
\end{keybox}

\noindent Writing out the two scalar equations explicitly:

\begin{align}
  \log\mathrm{IP}_{t}
  &= b_{11}\,\varepsilon_{t}^{\mathrm{FP}}
   + b_{12}\,\varepsilon_{t}^{\mathrm{MP}}
   + b_{13}\,\varepsilon_{t}^{\mathrm{TFP}}
   + b_{14}\,\varepsilon_{t}^{\mathrm{Oil}}
   + b_{15}\,\varepsilon_{t}^{\mathrm{Crisis}}
   + \omega_{1,t},\label{eq:IP_eq}\\[8pt]
  u_{t}
  &= b_{21}\,\varepsilon_{t}^{\mathrm{FP}}
   + b_{22}\,\varepsilon_{t}^{\mathrm{MP}}
   + b_{23}\,\varepsilon_{t}^{\mathrm{TFP}}
   + b_{24}\,\varepsilon_{t}^{\mathrm{Oil}}
   + b_{25}\,\varepsilon_{t}^{\mathrm{Crisis}}
   + \omega_{2,t}.\label{eq:u_eq}
\end{align}

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Complete Chain in One Display}
% ─────────────────────────────────────────────

Putting all three matrices together in one equation:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:full_chain}
  \underbrace{
  \begin{pmatrix} y_{1,t}\\[6pt] y_{2,t} \end{pmatrix}
  }_{2\times1}
  =
  \underbrace{
  \begin{pmatrix}
    a_{11} & a_{12} & a_{13} & a_{14} & a_{15}\\[4pt]
    a_{21} & a_{22} & a_{23} & a_{24} & a_{25}
  \end{pmatrix}
  }_{A_t:\;2\times5}
  \underbrace{
  \begin{pmatrix}
    \omega_{11} & \omega_{12} & \omega_{13} & \omega_{14} & \omega_{15}\\[4pt]
    \omega_{21} & \omega_{22} & \omega_{23} & \omega_{24} & \omega_{25}\\[4pt]
    \omega_{31} & \omega_{32} & \omega_{33} & \omega_{34} & \omega_{35}\\[4pt]
    \omega_{41} & \omega_{42} & \omega_{43} & \omega_{44} & \omega_{45}\\[4pt]
    \omega_{51} & \omega_{52} & \omega_{53} & \omega_{54} & \omega_{55}
  \end{pmatrix}
  }_{\Omega:\;5\times5}
  \underbrace{
  \begin{pmatrix}
    \varepsilon_{t}^{\mathrm{FP}}\\[4pt]
    \varepsilon_{t}^{\mathrm{MP}}\\[4pt]
    \varepsilon_{t}^{\mathrm{TFP}}\\[4pt]
    \varepsilon_{t}^{\mathrm{Oil}}\\[4pt]
    \varepsilon_{t}^{\mathrm{Crisis}}
  \end{pmatrix}
  }_{\bm{\varepsilon}_t:\;5\times1}
  +
  \bm{\omega}_t
\end{equation}

